==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*sebun}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*septḿ̥}}.
Cognate with Old High German [[sibun]] (German [[sieben]]), Old Saxon ''siƀun'' (Dutch [[zeven]]), Old Norse [[sjau]] (Swedish [[sju]]), Gothic [[𐍃𐌹𐌱𐌿𐌽]]; and, outside the Germanic languages, with Sanskrit {{m|sa|sc=Deva|सप्तन्|tr=saptán}}, Ancient Greek [[ἑπτά]], Latin [[septem]] (French [[sept]] etc.), Albanian [[shtatë]], Old Irish [[secht]] (Irish [[seacht]]), Old Church Slavonic [[сєдмь]].

